      "text": "Let's consider the given problem where we need to find the smallest positive integer \\( n \\), such that \\( n \\) is both an integer power of 11 and a palindromic number.\n\nFirst, let’s represent an integer power of 11 as:\n\\[\nA = 11^k\n\\]\nwhere \\( A \\) is an integer and \\( k \\) is a positive integer.\n\nNext, we need to determine which values of \\( k \\) yield palindromic numbers. A palindromic number reads the same forwards and backwards. This becomes our criterion for the smallest \\( n \\).\n\nLet's check the first few powers of 11:\n\n\\[\n11^1 = 11\n\\]\n\nThis number \\( 11 \\) is a one-digit number and thus is a palindrome. This is the smallest power of 11.\n\nTo check if there could be a smaller number, we consider \\( 11^2 \\):\n\n\\[\n11^2 = 121\n\\]\n\nThis number \\( 121 \\) is not palindromic.\n\nNext, let's calculate \\( 11^3 \\):\n\n\\[\n11^3 = 1331\n\\]\n\nThis number \\( 1331 \\) is also not palindromic.\n\nContinuing this manner, we examine additional values of \\( 11^4 \\):\n\n\\[\n11^4 = 14641\n\\]\n\nAnd \\( 11^5 \\):\n\n\\[\n11^5 = 161051\n\\]\n\nNone of the results for higher values of \\( k \\) seems to result in a palindromic number. The first palindromic values that turn up for \\( 11^j \\) start from:\n\n\\[\n11^2 = 121\n\\]\n\nThus, the smallest positive integer that is both an integer power of 11 and a palindromic number is:\n\n \n\\[\n\\boxed{121}\n\\]\n\nA house in the south attracts 0.25 times more birds than a house in the north. If the south house has 15 indiividuals each bird frequently eats half a cup of berries from the yard, and if the bird population in the north yard is 2 times the bird population in the south yard, how many berries will four birds from the north yard consume? Given the ratio ( a ) that the number of birds in the south is to the number of birds in the north as:\n[ 0.25:1 ]\n\nLet ( n ) be the number of birds in the north. Consequently, the number of birds in the south is:\n[ 0.25n ]\n\nWe know that the ratio of the number of berries consumed per bird in the south to the number of berries consumed per bird in the north is:\n[ 0.5 text{ cups}/text{bird} ]\n\nThe total number of berries consumed per bird in the south is:\n[ 0.25n times 0.5 text{ cups}/text{bird} ]\n\nSince each bird consumes the same amount of berries, the total number of berries consumed by an entire flock in the south is:\n[ 0.25n times 0.5 times 4 = 0.5n text{ cups} ]\n\nThe total number of berries consumed by an entire flock in the north is:\n[ n times 0.5 times 4 = n text{ cups} ]\n\nThe difference ( df ) in the total number of berries consumed per bird in the south compared to per bird in the north is:\n[ 0.5 text{ cups}/text{bird} ]\n\nTherefore, the total number of additional berries consumed by the birds in the south over the birds in the north is:\n[ (0.5 times 4)n = (0.5 times 4)n = n text{ cups} ]\n\nThe combined number of additional berries consumed by all birds is:\n[ 0.5n + n = 1.5n ]\n\nWhen simplifying, our answer is:\n[ n = 0.5n times 2 = n ]\n\nThus, the calculation correctly wraps up the solution.\n```",
